---
title: "Configuration"
description: "src/config.ts is the only knob in the system, why changing it recomputes history, and the rebuild trap that silently ignores your edit."
---

`src/config.ts` is 114 lines and it is the entire configuration surface of the
product. There is no admin UI, no settings table, no environment variable that
changes behaviour, and no runtime override. FR-25.

## What lives there

| Constant | Value today | What it controls |
|---|---|---|
| `GUILDS` | 9 entries | Slug, display name and **member count** for each competing guild |
| `TIER_MINUTES` | 22 / 45 / 75 / 0 | Minutes credited per tier |
| `TIER_ORDER` | short, medium, long, rest | Keyboard order. Rest last, matching the mockup |
| `WEEKLY_TARGET_MINUTES` | 150 | The personal target (WHO guideline) |
| `COMPETITION_START` / `COMPETITION_END` | `2026-07-27` / `2026-09-20` | **Placeholders.** See below |
| `TIMEZONE` | `Europe/Helsinki` | One timezone for the whole competition, not one per user |
| `MONDAY_POST_HOUR` | 9 | When the weekly group post goes out |
| `FOLLOWUP_AFTER_IGNORES` | 5 | FR-22's threshold |
| `REMINDER_GRACE_HOURS` | 2 | How late a missed reminder may still be delivered |
| `MAX_REMINDERS_PER_TICK` | 25 | Send pacing |

## Why an edit here recomputes all of history

Because nothing derived from these values is ever stored.

`days.tier` holds the string `"short"`. It does not hold 22. The conversion from
tier to minutes happens inside every read query, through a CTE built from
`TIER_MINUTES` on each call (`src/db/tiers.ts`). The same is true of member
counts: `standings()` divides by `guilds.member_count`, which `syncGuilds()`
overwrites from `config.ts` on every boot.

So correcting a member count that was wrong all season fixes **the whole season**,
retroactively, including the numbers already published in pinned messages, the
moment the next refresh runs. Changing a tier's minute value does the same. This
is the practical payoff of `SPEC.md` §4.4, and it is why the next-season runbook
is short.

::: {.callout-important}
## The corollary

The reason this works is that no cache, column or snapshot table anywhere holds a
sum. Adding one, even as an optimisation, breaks the property quietly: history
would then be partly stored and partly derived, and a config edit would produce
two numbers that disagree. There is no scale problem here that would justify it
(a few hundred users over eight weeks), so there is no case for the trade.
:::

## The two constants that are not really configuration

`FOLLOWUP_AFTER_IGNORES` is a constant rather than a setting because FR-22 *states*
the number five. A deployment running a different value would not be running this
competition's rules.

`TIMEZONE` is single because per-user timezones were rejected (`SPEC.md` §8). The
competition is between guilds at one university in one city.

## The placeholder dates

```ts
export const COMPETITION_START = "2026-07-27";
export const COMPETITION_END   = "2026-09-20";
```

These are **not real dates**. `SPEC.md` §9 Q1 is unresolved: the actual competition
dates were still under discussion when this was written. 2026-07-27 is a Monday
and 2026-09-20 is the Sunday eight weeks later, inclusive, chosen only to be a
plausible shape.

A window in the past or the future silently makes every log count for nothing,
with no error anywhere, because FR-26 excludes anything outside it. A test guards
this by asserting that today falls inside the window, **so the suite will start
failing once the placeholder expires.** That failure is the reminder working as
intended, not a broken test.

## The rebuild trap

::: {.callout-warning}
## `docker compose restart bot` does not pick up a config change

The `Dockerfile` does `COPY src ./src` at build time and nothing bind-mounts
`src`. A plain restart silently runs the previous configuration, with no error and
no log line saying so.

Any change under `src/` needs:

```sh
docker compose up -d --build
```

This has caught people. If a config edit appears to have done nothing, this is the
first thing to check.
:::

## Adding or removing a guild

`GUILDS` is the roster and `syncGuilds()` mirrors it into the `guilds` table on
every boot, upserting name and member count by slug. So:

- **Adding a guild** is one entry plus a restart. Its deep link
  `https://t.me/<bot>?startgroup=<slug>` starts working immediately.
- **Renaming** a guild is a display-name edit. The slug is the key and must not
  change, because it is in circulation inside callback payloads and deep links.
- **Removing** a guild is the awkward case. `syncGuilds` only ever inserts and
  updates, so a removed entry leaves its `guilds` row in place. Users and chats
  carry foreign keys onto it, so the row must stay unless you also move them. The
  handlers already tolerate a slug that is no longer in config: `bind()` in
  `src/bot/group.ts:43-55` answers with `BIND_GUILD_GONE` rather than failing, and
  the registration picker simply stops offering it.
